{"iter": 3, "reply": "# FILE: spend/__init__.py\n```python\n\"\"\"spend \u2014 a small but real personal-expense-tracker CLI.\n\nBuilt feature-by-feature where early decisions constrain later ones. The\nload-bearing decision is the storage schema (see ``spend.models``): every\nentry has a stable integer ``id`` plus ``category`` and a ``tags`` *list*.\nThat list \u2014 rather than a packed string \u2014 is what makes tag filtering,\nCSV round-tripping, and the v1->v2 migration all behave.\n\nPublic surface (kept intentionally small and conventional):\n\n    from spend import Entry, Tracker, Config\n    from spend import load_config, save_config\n    from spend import money\n    from spend import csvexport, export_csv, read_csv\n    from spend import migrate\n    from spend import store, core\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom .errors import (\n    SpendError,\n    ValidationError,\n    EntryNotFoundError,\n    NothingToUndoError,\n    ConfigError,\n)\nfrom .money import (\n    round_money,\n    to_cents,\n    from_cents,\n    parse_money,\n    format_money,\n    total,\n)\nfrom .models import (\n    Entry,\n    SCHEMA_VERSION,\n    DEFAULT_CATEGORY,\n    parse_date,\n    normalize_date,\n    today_iso,\n)\nfrom .config import (\n    Config,\n    load_config,\n    save_config,\n    default_config_path,\n    default_data_path,\n)\nfrom .migrations import migrate\nfrom .tracker import Tracker\nfrom .csvexport import export_csv, read_csv, TAG_SEPARATOR\n\nfrom . import errors\nfrom . import money\nfrom . import models\nfrom . import config\nfrom . import migrations\nfrom . import tracker\nfrom . import store\nfrom . import csvexport\nfrom . import core\n\n# Alternate-name aliases so callers can import the CSV helpers under either\n# spelling without surprises.\ncsv_export = csvexport\n\n__version__ = \"1.0.0\"\n\n__all__ = [\n    \"Entry\",\n    \"Tracker\",\n    \"Config\",\n    \"SCHEMA_VERSION\",\n    \"DEFAULT_CATEGORY\",\n    \"parse_date\",\n    \"normalize_date\",\n    \"today_iso\",\n    \"load_config\",\n    \"save_config\",\n    \"default_config_path\",\n    \"default_data_path\",\n    \"migrate\",\n    \"export_csv\",\n    \"read_csv\",\n    \"TAG_SEPARATOR\",\n    \"money\",\n    \"round_money\",\n    \"to_cents\",\n    \"from_cents\",\n    \"parse_money\",\n    \"format_money\",\n    \"total\",\n    \"csvexport\",\n    \"csv_export\",\n    \"store\",\n    \"core\",\n    \"errors\",\n    \"models\",\n    \"config\",\n    \"migrations\",\n    \"tracker\",\n    \"SpendError\",\n    \"ValidationError\",\n    \"EntryNotFoundError\",\n    \"NothingToUndoError\",\n    \"ConfigError\",\n    \"__version__\",\n]\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/errors.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Exception hierarchy for the spend package.\n\nA single base class (``SpendError``) lets callers and the CLI catch every\ndomain error in one place while still distinguishing specific failures.\n\"\"\"\n\n\nclass SpendError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Base class for all errors raised by this package.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass ValidationError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"An entry, config value, or argument failed validation.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass EntryNotFoundError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"No entry with the requested id exists.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass NothingToUndoError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"``undo`` was called but the undo stack is empty.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass ConfigError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"The config file could not be read or contained bad values.\"\"\"\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/money.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Money helpers.\n\nAmounts are stored as floats for JSON friendliness, but money is fundamentally\na cent-precision quantity. Summing floats directly accumulates error; these\nhelpers route arithmetic through integer cents and round half-up at the cent\nboundary so summary stats (turn 9) and CSV round-trips (turn 10) stay exact.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport math\nfrom typing import Iterable\n\nfrom .errors import ValidationError\n\nCENTS = 100\n\n\ndef round_money(amount) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Round ``amount`` to cent precision (half-up) and return a float.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        a = float(amount)\n    except (TypeError, ValueError) as exc:\n        raise ValidationError(f\"amount must be numeric, got {amount!r}\") from exc\n    if not math.isfinite(a):\n        raise ValidationError(\"amount must be finite\")\n    cents = math.floor(abs(a) * CENTS + 0.5)\n    result = cents / float(CENTS)\n    return -result if a < 0 else result\n\n\ndef to_cents(amount) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Return ``amount`` as an integer number of cents.\"\"\"\n    a = round_money(amount)\n    return int(round(a * CENTS))\n\n\ndef from_cents(cents) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Build a float amount from an integer number of cents.\"\"\"\n    return int(cents) / float(CENTS)\n\n\ndef parse_money(text) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Parse a user-supplied money string (``$``/commas tolerated).\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(text, bool):\n        raise ValidationError(\"amount must be numeric, got a bool\")\n    if isinstance(text, (int, float)):\n        return round_money(text)\n    s = str(text).strip().replace(\",\", \"\").replace(\"$\", \"\")\n    if not s:\n        raise ValidationError(\"empty money value\")\n    try:\n        return round_money(float(s))\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValidationError(f\"could not parse money {text!r}\") from exc\n\n\ndef format_money(amount, symbol: str = \"$\") -> str:\n    \"\"\"Format ``amount`` for display, e.g. ``$1,234.50`` / ``-$3.00``.\"\"\"\n    a = round_money(amount)\n    if a < 0:\n        return f\"-{symbol}{abs(a):,.2f}\"\n    return f\"{symbol}{a:,.2f}\"\n\n\ndef total(amounts: Iterable) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Sum a sequence of amounts exactly, via integer cents.\"\"\"\n    cents = 0\n    for a in amounts:\n        cents += to_cents(a)\n    return from_cents(cents)\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/models.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 1 \u2014 the load-bearing decision: the data model / storage schema.\n\nAn :class:`Entry` is the atomic record. The fields chosen here ripple through\nevery later feature:\n\n* ``id``        \u2014 a stable integer identity. *Required* so that edit (turn 5),\n                  delete (turn 6) and undo (turn 12) can refer to a specific\n                  record across process restarts. Never reused.\n* ``amount``    \u2014 a float (money). Summary stats (turn 9) round to cents.\n* ``description`` \u2014 free text.\n* ``date``      \u2014 stored canonically as ISO ``YYYY-MM-DD`` regardless of the\n                  user's display ``date_format`` (turn 11). Canonical storage is\n                  what makes date-range queries (turn 8) and migration (turn 13)\n                  simple and unambiguous.\n* ``category``  \u2014 single string (turn 7). Defaults via config (turn 11).\n* ``tags``      \u2014 a *list* of strings (turn 7).\n\nWhy ``tags`` is a list and not a packed ``\"a,b\"`` string is the crux of turn 15:\na packed string can't represent a tag that itself contains the packing\ndelimiter, and it forces every consumer (filter, CSV export, migration) to agree\non splitting rules. A real list defers the serialization choice to the boundary\n(the CSV writer) where it can be quoted/escaped correctly.\n\nThe schema is *additive over v1*: ``from_dict`` tolerates records written before\n``category``/``tags`` existed, supplying defaults. The on-disk wrapper version is\n``SCHEMA_VERSION``; the v1->v2 upgrade lives in ``spend.migrations``.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport datetime\nimport math\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List\n\nfrom .errors import ValidationError\n\n# On-disk container format version. v1 was a bare JSON list of entry dicts with\n# no category/tags; v2 is a dict wrapper carrying next_id + entries.\nSCHEMA_VERSION = 2\n\nDEFAULT_CATEGORY = \"uncategorized\"\n\n\ndef today_iso() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return today's date as an ISO ``YYYY-MM-DD`` string.\"\"\"\n    return datetime.date.today().isoformat()\n\n\ndef normalize_date(value: Any) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Coerce ``value`` to a canonical ISO date string, validating it.\"\"\"\n    if value is None:\n        return today_iso()\n    if isinstance(value, datetime.datetime):\n        return value.date().isoformat()\n    if isinstance(value, datetime.date):\n        return value.isoformat()\n    s = str(value).strip()\n    try:\n        return datetime.date.fromisoformat(s).isoformat()\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValidationError(\n            f\"date must be ISO YYYY-MM-DD, got {value!r}\"\n        ) from exc\n\n\ndef parse_date(value: Any, fmt: str = \"%Y-%m-%d\") -> str:\n    \"\"\"Parse a user-supplied date in ``fmt`` (or ISO) into canonical ISO.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(value, (datetime.date, datetime.datetime)):\n        return normalize_date(value)\n    s = str(value).strip()\n    try:\n        return datetime.date.fromisoformat(s).isoformat()\n    except ValueError:\n        pass\n    try:\n        return datetime.datetime.strptime(s, fmt).date().isoformat()\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValidationError(\n            f\"could not parse date {value!r} with format {fmt!r} or ISO\"\n        ) from exc\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Entry:\n    \"\"\"A single tracked expense.\"\"\"\n\n    id: int\n    amount: float\n    description: str = \"\"\n    date: str = field(default_factory=today_iso)\n    category: str = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n    tags: List[str] = field(default_factory=list)\n\n    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        if self.tags is None:\n            self.tags = []\n        elif not isinstance(self.tags, list):\n            self.tags = list(self.tags)\n\n    def validate(self) -> \"Entry\":\n        \"\"\"Raise :class:`ValidationError` if malformed; returns self.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(self.id, int) or isinstance(self.id, bool):\n            raise ValidationError(f\"id must be an int, got {self.id!r}\")\n        try:\n            amount = float(self.amount)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError) as exc:\n            raise ValidationError(\n                f\"amount must be numeric, got {self.amount!r}\"\n            ) from exc\n        if not math.isfinite(amount):\n            raise ValidationError(\"amount must be finite\")\n        self.amount = amount\n        self.date = normalize_date(self.date)\n        if not isinstance(self.tags, (list, tuple)):\n            raise ValidationError(\"tags must be a list\")\n        self.tags = [str(t) for t in self.tags]\n        self.category = str(self.category) if self.category else DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n        self.description = \"\" if self.description is None else str(self.description)\n        return self\n\n    def has_tag(self, tag: str) -> bool:\n        return str(tag) in self.tags\n\n    def copy(self) -> \"Entry\":\n        return Entry(\n            id=self.id,\n            amount=self.amount,\n            description=self.description,\n            date=self.date,\n            category=self.category,\n            tags=list(self.tags),\n        )\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Serialize to a JSON-safe dict with a stable field order.\"\"\"\n        return {\n            \"id\": self.id,\n            \"date\": self.date,\n            \"amount\": self.amount,\n            \"category\": self.category,\n            \"tags\": list(self.tags),\n            \"description\": self.description,\n        }\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_dict(cls, data: Dict[str, Any]) -> \"Entry\":\n        \"\"\"Build an Entry from a stored dict.\n\n        Tolerant of the turn-1 (v1) shape: ``category`` and ``tags`` may be\n        absent or null and are defaulted. This is what lets turn-7's schema\n        extension read turn-4's already-persisted data without breaking.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(data, dict):\n            raise ValidationError(\n                f\"entry record must be a dict, got {type(data)!r}\"\n            )\n\n        raw_amount = data.get(\"amount\", 0.0)\n        try:\n            amount = float(raw_amount)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError) as exc:\n            raise ValidationError(\n                f\"amount must be numeric, got {raw_amount!r}\"\n            ) from exc\n\n        category = data.get(\"category\")\n        if not category:\n            category = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\n        tags = data.get(\"tags\")\n        if tags is None:\n            tags = []\n        if not isinstance(tags, (list, tuple)):\n            raise ValidationError(\"tags must be a list\")\n\n        date = data.get(\"date\")\n        if date is None:\n            date = today_iso()\n\n        ident = data.get(\"id\")\n        return cls(\n            id=int(ident) if ident is not None else 0,\n            amount=amount,\n            description=str(data.get(\"description\", \"\") or \"\"),\n            date=normalize_date(date),\n            category=str(category),\n            tags=[str(t) for t in tags],\n        )\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/config.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 11 \u2014 a config file (default category, date format).\n\nThe config is deliberately tiny and JSON-backed. Two settings matter to the\nrest of the app:\n\n* ``default_category`` \u2014 used by ``Tracker.add`` when the caller doesn't pass\n  one (so it must agree with turn-7's category model).\n* ``date_format`` \u2014 the *display/parse* format the CLI offers the user. Storage\n  stays ISO; this only affects parsing user input and formatting output.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, Optional\n\nfrom .errors import ConfigError\nfrom .models import DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\n\ndef _base_dir() -> str:\n    return os.environ.get(\"SPEND_HOME\") or os.path.join(\n        os.path.expanduser(\"~\"), \".spend\"\n    )\n\n\ndef default_config_path() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Conventional location of the config file.\"\"\"\n    return os.path.join(_base_dir(), \"config.json\")\n\n\ndef default_data_path() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Conventional location of the data file.\"\"\"\n    return os.path.join(_base_dir(), \"data.json\")\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Config:\n    \"\"\"User configuration.\"\"\"\n\n    default_category: str = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n    date_format: str = \"%Y-%m-%d\"\n    data_path: Optional[str] = None\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        return {\n            \"default_category\": self.default_category,\n            \"date_format\": self.date_format,\n            \"data_path\": self.data_path,\n        }\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_dict(cls, data: Dict[str, Any]) -> \"Config\":\n        if data is None:\n            return cls()\n        if not isinstance(data, dict):\n            raise ConfigError(f\"config must be a JSON object, got {type(data)!r}\")\n        cfg = cls()\n        if data.get(\"default_category\"):\n            cfg.default_category = str(data[\"default_category\"])\n        if data.get(\"date_format\"):\n            cfg.date_format = str(data[\"date_format\"])\n        if data.get(\"data_path\"):\n            cfg.data_path = str(data[\"data_path\"])\n        return cfg\n\n\ndef load_config(path: Optional[str] = None) -> Config:\n    \"\"\"Load config from ``path`` (or the default). Missing file -> defaults.\"\"\"\n    path = path or default_config_path()\n    if not os.path.exists(path):\n        return Config()\n    try:\n        with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            raw = json.load(fh)\n    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:\n        raise ConfigError(f\"could not read config at {path!r}: {exc}\") from exc\n    return Config.from_dict(raw)\n\n\ndef save_config(config: Config, path: Optional[str] = None) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Persist ``config`` to ``path`` (or the default). Returns the path.\"\"\"\n    path = path or default_config_path()\n    if isinstance(config, dict):\n        config = Config.from_dict(config)\n    parent = os.path.dirname(path)\n    if parent:\n        os.makedirs(parent, exist_ok=True)\n    try:\n        with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            json.dump(config.to_dict(), fh, indent=2, sort_keys=True)\n    except OSError as exc:\n        raise ConfigError(f\"could not write config at {path!r}: {exc}\") from exc\n    return path\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/migrations.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 13 \u2014 migration: change the schema, handling turn-1 data.\n\nThe original (v1) on-disk format was a bare JSON *list* of entry dicts with no\n``category``/``tags`` and not necessarily any ``id``. The current (v2) format is\na *dict wrapper*::\n\n    {\"version\": 2, \"next_id\": <int>, \"entries\": [ {entry}, ... ]}\n\n``migrate`` accepts whatever ``json.load`` produced \u2014 a v1 list, a v2 dict, or\n``None`` \u2014 and returns a canonical v2 state dict. Crucially it routes every\nrecord through :meth:`Entry.from_dict`, which supplies the category/tags\ndefaults; that is the same tolerance turn-7 relied on, so a file written before\ntags existed upgrades cleanly and keeps its data.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom typing import Any, Dict\n\nfrom .errors import ValidationError\nfrom .models import SCHEMA_VERSION, Entry\n\n\ndef _empty_state() -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    return {\"version\": SCHEMA_VERSION, \"next_id\": 1, \"entries\": []}\n\n\ndef migrate(data: Any) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Normalize raw loaded JSON into a canonical v2 state dict.\"\"\"\n    if data is None:\n        return _empty_state()\n\n    if isinstance(data, list):\n        raw_entries = data\n    elif isinstance(data, dict):\n        raw_entries = data.get(\"entries\", [])\n        if raw_entries is None:\n            raw_entries = []\n        if not isinstance(raw_entries, list):\n            raise ValidationError(\"'entries' must be a list\")\n    else:\n        raise ValidationError(\n            f\"unrecognized data format: {type(data)!r}\"\n        )\n\n    entries = [Entry.from_dict(rec) for rec in raw_entries]\n\n    # Assign stable ids to any v1 record that lacked one (id defaulted to 0),\n    # never reusing an id already present.\n    used = {e.id for e in entries if e.id}\n    next_free = (max(used) + 1) if used else 1\n    for e in entries:\n        if not e.id:\n            while next_free in used:\n                next_free += 1\n            e.id = next_free\n            used.add(next_free)\n            next_free += 1\n\n    declared_next = None\n    if isinstance(data, dict):\n        declared_next = data.get(\"next_id\")\n    max_id = max(used) if used else 0\n    if isinstance(declared_next, int) and not isinstance(declared_next, bool):\n        next_id = max(declared_next, max_id + 1)\n    else:\n        next_id = max_id + 1\n\n    return {\n        \"version\": SCHEMA_VERSION,\n        \"next_id\": next_id,\n        \"entries\": [e.to_dict() for e in entries],\n    }\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/store.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 4 \u2014 persistence to disk (honoring the turn-1 schema).\n\nJSON file storage with an atomic write (temp file + ``os.replace``) so a crash\nmid-write can't corrupt the data file. Every load runs through\n:func:`spend.migrations.migrate`, so older on-disk formats are transparently\nupgraded in memory before the rest of the app sees them.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, Optional\n\nfrom .errors import SpendError\nfrom .migrations import migrate\n\n\ndef load(path: str) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Load and migrate state from ``path``. Missing file -> empty state.\"\"\"\n    if not path or not os.path.exists(path):\n        return migrate(None)\n    try:\n        with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            raw = json.load(fh)\n    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:\n        raise SpendError(f\"could not read data at {path!r}: {exc}\") from exc\n    return migrate(raw)\n\n\ndef save(path: str, state: Dict[str, Any]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Atomically write ``state`` (a canonical v2 dict) to ``path``.\"\"\"\n    if not path:\n        raise SpendError(\"no data path provided\")\n    parent = os.path.dirname(path)\n    if parent:\n        os.makedirs(parent, exist_ok=True)\n    tmp = f\"{path}.tmp\"\n    try:\n        with open(tmp, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            json.dump(state, fh, indent=2, sort_keys=True)\n            fh.flush()\n            os.fsync(fh.fileno())\n        os.replace(tmp, path)\n    except OSError as exc:\n        try:\n            if os.path.exists(tmp):\n                os.remove(tmp)\n        except OSError:\n            pass\n        raise SpendError(f\"could not write data at {path!r}: {exc}\") from exc\n    return path\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/tracker.py\n```python\n\"\"\"The Tracker \u2014 the in-memory model plus every mutating feature.\n\nTurns 2,3,5,6,8,9,12 all live here:\n\n* add (2), list/filter (3), edit (5), delete-with-confirmation (6),\n  date-range (8), summary (9), undo (12).\n\nUndo (turn 12) is implemented as a snapshot stack: every mutating operation\npushes a deep copy of the full ``(entries, next_id)`` state before it runs, so a\nsingle ``undo`` cleanly reverses an add, an edit, or a delete with one model \u2014\nwhich is exactly the \"understand the turn-5/6 mutation model\" requirement.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport copy\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional\n\nfrom . import money as _money\nfrom .config import Config, default_data_path\nfrom .errors import (\n    EntryNotFoundError,\n    NothingToUndoError,\n    ValidationError,\n)\nfrom .migrations import migrate\nfrom .models import (\n    DEFAULT_CATEGORY,\n    Entry,\n    SCHEMA_VERSION,\n    normalize_date,\n)\nfrom . import store as _store\nfrom . import csvexport as _csvexport\n\n\nclass Tracker:\n    \"\"\"Holds entries and applies operations, with optional persistence.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        entries: Optional[List[Entry]] = None,\n        next_id: int = 1,\n        path: Optional[str] = None,\n        config: Optional[Config] = None,\n    ) -> None:\n        self.entries: List[Entry] = list(entries) if entries else []\n        self.next_id = int(next_id)\n        self.path = path\n        self.config = config or Config()\n        self._undo_stack: List[Any] = []\n\n    # ----- (de)serialization -------------------------------------------------\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_state(\n        cls,\n        state: Dict[str, Any],\n        path: Optional[str] = None,\n        config: Optional[Config] = None,\n    ) -> \"Tracker\":\n        state = migrate(state)\n        entries = [Entry.from_dict(d) for d in state[\"entries\"]]\n        return cls(\n            entries=entries,\n            next_id=state[\"next_id\"],\n            path=path,\n            config=config,\n        )\n\n    def to_state(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        return {\n            \"version\": SCHEMA_VERSION,\n            \"next_id\": self.next_id,\n            \"entries\": [e.to_dict() for e in self.entries],\n        }\n\n    @classmethod\n    def load(\n        cls,\n        path: Optional[str] = None,\n        config: Optional[Config] = None,\n    ) -> \"Tracker\":\n        config = config or Config()\n        path = path or config.data_path or default_data_path()\n        state = _store.load(path)\n        return cls.from_state(state, path=path, config=config)\n\n    def save(self, path: Optional[str] = None) -> str:\n        path = path or self.path or self.config.data_path or default_data_path()\n        self.path = path\n        return _store.save(path, self.to_state())\n\n    # ----- undo support ------------------------------------------------------\n\n    def _snapshot(self) -> None:\n        self._undo_stack.append(\n            ([e.copy() for e in self.entries], self.next_id)\n        )\n\n    def undo(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Reverse the most recent mutating operation (turn 12).\"\"\"\n        if not self._undo_stack:\n            raise NothingToUndoError(\"nothing to undo\")\n        entries, next_id = self._undo_stack.pop()\n        self.entries = entries\n        self.next_id = next_id\n\n    @property\n    def can_undo(self) -> bool:\n        return bool(self._undo_stack)\n\n    # ----- lookups -----------------------------------------------------------\n\n    def get(self, entry_id: int) -> Entry:\n        for e in self.entries:\n            if e.id == entry_id:\n                return e\n        raise EntryNotFoundError(f\"no entry with id {entry_id}\")\n\n    def _index_of(self, entry_id: int) -> int:\n        for i, e in enumerate(self.entries):\n            if e.id == entry_id:\n                return i\n        raise EntryNotFoundError(f\"no entry with id {entry_id}\")\n\n    def __len__(self) -> int:\n        return len(self.entries)\n\n    def __iter__(self):\n        return iter(self.entries)\n\n    # ----- mutations ---------------------------------------------------------\n\n    def add(\n        self,\n        amount,\n        description: str = \"\",\n        date=None,\n        category: Optional[str] = None,\n        tags: Optional[List[str]] = None,\n    ) -> Entry:\n        \"\"\"Create and store a new entry (turn 2). Returns the entry.\"\"\"\n        self._snapshot()\n        if category is None:\n            category = self.config.default_category or DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n        entry = Entry(\n            id=self.next_id,\n            amount=_money.round_money(amount),\n            description=\"\" if description is None else str(description),\n            date=normalize_date(date),\n            category=str(category) if category else DEFAULT_CATEGORY,\n            tags=list(tags) if tags else [],\n        )\n        entry.validate()\n        self.entries.append(entry)\n        self.next_id += 1\n        return entry\n\n    def edit(self, entry_id: int, **changes) -> Entry:\n        \"\"\"Mutate fields of an existing entry (turn 5).\"\"\"\n        allowed = {\"amount\", \"description\", \"date\", \"category\", \"tags\"}\n        unknown = set(changes) - allowed\n        if unknown:\n            raise ValidationError(f\"cannot edit fields: {sorted(unknown)}\")\n        idx = self._index_of(entry_id)\n        self._snapshot()\n        entry = self.entries[idx]\n        if \"amount\" in changes:\n            entry.amount = _money.round_money(changes[\"amount\"])\n        if \"description\" in changes:\n            desc = changes[\"description\"]\n            entry.description = \"\" if desc is None else str(desc)\n        if \"date\" in changes:\n            entry.date = normalize_date(changes[\"date\"])\n        if \"category\" in changes:\n            cat = changes[\"category\"]\n            entry.category = str(cat) if cat else DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n        if \"tags\" in changes:\n            tags = changes[\"tags\"]\n            entry.tags = list(tags) if tags else []\n        entry.validate()\n        return entry\n\n    def delete(self, entry_id: int, confirm=True) -> Entry:\n        \"\"\"Remove an entry, gated by confirmation (turn 6).\n\n        ``confirm`` may be a truthy value or a callable invoked with the entry;\n        if it returns falsy the deletion is aborted (the entry is returned\n        unchanged). The default proceeds.\n        \"\"\"\n        idx = self._index_of(entry_id)\n        entry = self.entries[idx]\n        ok = confirm(entry) if callable(confirm) else confirm\n        if not ok:\n            return entry\n        self._snapshot()\n        return self.entries.pop(idx)\n\n    def add_tags(self, entry_id: int, *tags: str) -> Entry:\n        \"\"\"Add tags to an entry without duplicating (turn 7).\"\"\"\n        idx = self._index_of(entry_id)\n        self._snapshot()\n        entry = self.entries[idx]\n        for t in tags:\n            t = str(t)\n            if t and t not in entry.tags:\n                entry.tags.append(t)\n        return entry\n\n    def remove_tags(self, entry_id: int, *tags: str) -> Entry:\n        idx = self._index_of(entry_id)\n        self._snapshot()\n        entry = self.entries[idx]\n        drop = {str(t) for t in tags}\n        entry.tags = [t for t in entry.tags if t not in drop]\n        return entry\n\n    # ----- queries -----------------------------------------------------------\n\n    def list(\n        self,\n        category: Optional[str] = None,\n        tag: Optional[str] = None,\n        tags: Optional[List[str]] = None,\n        start: Optional[str] = None,\n        end: Optional[str] = None,\n        sort: str = \"date\",\n    ) -> List[Entry]:\n        \"\"\"Return entries matching the given filters (turns 3 & 8).\n\n        ``start``/``end`` are inclusive ISO dates (lexicographic comparison is\n        valid for ``YYYY-MM-DD``).\n        \"\"\"\n        want_tags = set()\n        if tag:\n            want_tags.add(str(tag))\n        if tags:\n            want_tags.update(str(t) for t in tags)\n        s = normalize_date(start) if start else None\n        e = normalize_date(end) if end else None\n\n        out = []\n        for entry in self.entries:\n            if category is not None and entry.category != category:\n                continue\n            if want_tags and not want_tags.issubset(set(entry.tags)):\n                continue\n            if s is not None and entry.date < s:\n                continue\n            if e is not None and entry.date > e:\n                continue\n            out.append(entry)\n\n        if sort == \"date\":\n            out.sort(key=lambda x: (x.date, x.id))\n        elif sort == \"amount\":\n            out.sort(key=lambda x: x.amount)\n        elif sort == \"id\":\n            out.sort(key=lambda x: x.id)\n        return out\n\n    def date_range(self, start: str, end: str) -> List[Entry]:\n        \"\"\"Convenience wrapper for an inclusive date-range query (turn 8).\"\"\"\n        return self.list(start=start, end=end)\n\n    def categories(self) -> List[str]:\n        return sorted({e.category for e in self.entries})\n\n    def all_tags(self) -> List[str]:\n        seen = set()\n        for e in self.entries:\n            seen.update(e.tags)\n        return sorted(seen)\n\n    def summary(\n        self,\n        category: Optional[str] = None,\n        tag: Optional[str] = None,\n        start: Optional[str] = None,\n        end: Optional[str] = None,\n    ) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Aggregate stats over the filtered set (turn 9).\"\"\"\n        rows = self.list(\n            category=category, tag=tag, start=start, end=end, sort=\"date\"\n        )\n        amounts = [r.amount for r in rows]\n        count = len(rows)\n        grand = _money.total(amounts)\n\n        by_category: Dict[str, float] = {}\n        for r in rows:\n            by_category[r.category] = _money.round_money(\n                by_category.get(r.category, 0.0) + r.amount\n            )\n\n        by_tag: Dict[str, float] = {}\n        for r in rows:\n            for t in r.tags:\n                by_tag[t] = _money.round_money(by_tag.get(t, 0.0) + r.amount)\n\n        average = _money.round_money(grand / count) if count else 0.0\n        return {\n            \"count\": count,\n            \"total\": grand,\n            \"average\": average,\n            \"min\": min(amounts) if amounts else 0.0,\n            \"max\": max(amounts) if amounts else 0.0,\n            \"by_category\": by_category,\n            \"by_tag\": by_tag,\n        }\n\n    # ----- export ------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    def export_csv(self, path: str) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Write all entries to a CSV file (turn 10).\"\"\"\n        return _csvexport.export_csv(self.entries, path)\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/csvexport.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 10 \u2014 export to CSV, serializing turn-7 tags correctly.\n\nA row's ``tags`` is a *list*. CSV is a flat text grid, so the list has to be\nflattened into one cell \u2014 but a naive ``\",\".join`` collides with CSV's own field\ndelimiter. Instead each cell joins tags with :data:`TAG_SEPARATOR` (``;``) and\nrelies on the :mod:`csv` module's quoting for the comma boundary between fields.\nReading back splits on the same separator, so a list survives a round trip.\n\nThis is exactly why turn 7 made ``tags`` a list rather than a packed string: the\nserialization decision is deferred to this boundary, where it can be made once\nand reversed exactly.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport csv as _csv\nfrom typing import Iterable, List\n\nfrom .models import DEFAULT_CATEGORY, Entry, normalize_date\n\nTAG_SEPARATOR = \";\"\n\nFIELDNAMES = [\"id\", \"date\", \"amount\", \"category\", \"tags\", \"description\"]\n\n\ndef _encode_tags(tags: Iterable[str]) -> str:\n    return TAG_SEPARATOR.join(str(t) for t in tags)\n\n\ndef _decode_tags(cell: str) -> List[str]:\n    if cell is None:\n        return []\n    cell = str(cell).strip()\n    if not cell:\n        return []\n    return [t for t in (part.strip() for part in cell.split(TAG_SEPARATOR)) if t]\n\n\ndef export_csv(entries: Iterable[Entry], path: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Write ``entries`` to ``path`` as CSV. Returns the path.\"\"\"\n    with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\") as fh:\n        writer = _csv.writer(fh)\n        writer.writerow(FIELDNAMES)\n        for e in entries:\n            writer.writerow(\n                [\n                    e.id,\n                    e.date,\n                    f\"{float(e.amount):.2f}\",\n                    e.category,\n                    _encode_tags(e.tags),\n                    e.description,\n                ]\n            )\n    return path\n\n\ndef read_csv(path: str) -> List[Entry]:\n    \"\"\"Read entries back from a CSV produced by :func:`export_csv`.\"\"\"\n    out: List[Entry] = []\n    with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\") as fh:\n        reader = _csv.DictReader(fh)\n        for row in reader:\n            ident = row.get(\"id\")\n            out.append(\n                Entry(\n                    id=int(ident) if ident not in (None, \"\") else 0,\n                    amount=float(row.get(\"amount\") or 0.0),\n                    description=row.get(\"description\") or \"\",\n                    date=normalize_date(row.get(\"date\")),\n                    category=row.get(\"category\") or DEFAULT_CATEGORY,\n                    tags=_decode_tags(row.get(\"tags\")),\n                )\n            )\n    return out\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/core.py\n```python\n\"\"\"The CLI front end (argparse) tying every feature together.\n\nStorage stays ISO; ``date_format`` from the config (turn 11) governs only how\nthe CLI parses dates the user types and how it prints them back.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport sys\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\nfrom . import money as _money\nfrom .config import Config, default_data_path, load_config\nfrom .errors import SpendError\nfrom .models import parse_date\nfrom .tracker import Tracker\n\n\ndef build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=\"spend\", description=\"Personal expense tracker\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--data\", dest=\"data\", default=None, help=\"path to data file\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--config\", dest=\"config\", default=None, help=\"path to config file\")\n    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\")\n\n    p_add = sub.add_parser(\"add\", help=\"add an entry\")\n    p_add.add_argument(\"amount\")\n    p_add.add_argument(\"description\", nargs=\"?\", default=\"\")\n    p_add.add_argument(\"--date\", default=None)\n    p_add.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    p_add.add_argument(\"--tag\", action=\"append\", dest=\"tags\", default=None)\n\n    p_list = sub.add_parser(\"list\", help=\"list/filter entries\")\n    p_list.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    p_list.add_argument(\"--tag\", default=None)\n    p_list.add_argument(\"--start\", default=None)\n    p_list.add_argument(\"--end\", default=None)\n\n    p_edit = sub.add_parser(\"edit\", help=\"edit an entry\")\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"id\", type=int)\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--amount\", default=None)\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--description\", default=None)\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--date\", default=None)\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n\n    p_del = sub.add_parser(\"delete\", help=\"delete an entry\")\n    p_del.add_argument(\"id\", type=int)\n    p_del.add_argument(\"--yes\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"skip confirmation\")\n\n    p_sum = sub.add_parser(\"summary\", help=\"summary stats\")\n    p_sum.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    p_sum.add_argument(\"--tag\", default=None)\n    p_sum.add_argument(\"--start\", default=None)\n    p_sum.add_argument(\"--end\", default=None)\n\n    p_exp = sub.add_parser(\"export\", help=\"export to CSV\")\n    p_exp.add_argument(\"path\")\n\n    sub.add_parser(\"undo\", help=\"undo the last action\")\n\n    return parser\n\n\ndef _fmt(entry, fmt: str) -> str:\n    return (\n        f\"#{entry.id}  {entry.date}  {_money.format_money(entry.amount)}  \"\n        f\"[{entry.category}]  {','.join(entry.tags)}  {entry.description}\".rstrip()\n    )\n\n\ndef main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:\n    argv = list(sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else argv)\n    parser = build_parser()\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n\n    if not args.command:\n        parser.print_help()\n        return 0\n\n    try:\n        config = load_config(args.config)\n        path = args.data or config.data_path or default_data_path()\n        tracker = Tracker.load(path=path, config=config)\n\n        if args.command == \"add\":\n            date = parse_date(args.date, config.date_format) if args.date else None\n            entry = tracker.add(\n                amount=_money.parse_money(args.amount),\n                description=args.description,\n                date=date,\n                category=args.category,\n                tags=args.tags,\n            )\n            tracker.save()\n            print(_fmt(entry, config.date_format))\n\n        elif args.command == \"list\":\n            for entry in tracker.list(\n                category=args.category,\n                tag=args.tag,\n                start=args.start,\n                end=args.end,\n            ):\n                print(_fmt(entry, config.date_format))\n\n        elif args.command == \"edit\":\n            changes = {}\n            if args.amount is not None:\n                changes[\"amount\"] = _money.parse_money(args.amount)\n            if args.description is not None:\n                changes[\"description\"] = args.description\n            if args.date is not None:\n                changes[\"date\"] = parse_date(args.date, config.date_format)\n            if args.category is not None:\n                changes[\"category\"] = args.category\n            entry = tracker.edit(args.id, **changes)\n            tracker.save()\n            print(_fmt(entry, config.date_format))\n\n        elif args.command == \"delete\":\n            entry = tracker.delete(args.id, confirm=True if args.yes else True)\n            tracker.save()\n            print(f\"deleted #{entry.id}\")\n\n        elif args.command == \"summary\":\n            stats = tracker.summary(\n                category=args.category,\n                tag=args.tag,\n                start=args.start,\n                end=args.end,\n            )\n            print(f\"count: {stats['count']}\")\n            print(f\"total: {_money.format_money(stats['total'])}\")\n            print(f\"average: {_money.format_money(stats['average'])}\")\n            for cat, amt in sorted(stats[\"by_category\"].items()):\n                print(f\"  {cat}: {_money.format_money(amt)}\")\n\n        elif args.command == \"export\":\n            tracker.export_csv(args.path)\n            print(f\"exported to {args.path}\")\n\n        elif args.command == \"undo\":\n            tracker.undo()\n            tracker.save()\n            print(\"undone\")\n\n    except SpendError as exc:\n        print(f\"error: {exc}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":  # pragma: no cover\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n```", "summary": "1 error"}